            <ArticleTitle>Protracted febrile myalgia in patients with familial Mediterranean fever.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1708-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We describe a newly defined syndrome of protracted febrile myalgia in patients with familial Mediterranean fever (FMF).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fourteen patients with FMF were admitted with an attack of severe disabling myalgia accompanied by fever, high erythrocyte sedimentation rate, and hyperglobulinemia, lasting up to 6 weeks.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Unlike in the classical manifestations of FMF response to corticosteroids therapy was prompt.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Protracted febrile myalgia is an uncommon dramatic manifestation of FMF that may occur despite colchicine therapy and requires treatment with corticosteroids.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
